lds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>TEN \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>TEN are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>38%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 TEN project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Tony Pollard</strong>'s rushing projection (21 carries, 96 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Wan'Dale Robinson</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, TEN funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>10 targets for 67 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Gunnar Helm</strong> (TE, 5 targets, 31 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 4-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p><